1
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:23,700
 Stars, galaxies, in astral seas above, on cosmic paths we'll trace.

2
00:00:24,020 --> 00:01:32,990
 Man's overwankity is such a wonder thing.

3
00:01:33,750 --> 00:01:40,090
 Be hot, do solve, come see, come take your place.

4
00:01:40,390 --> 00:01:55,930
 We're leaving soon for...

5
00:01:55,930 --> 00:02:12,420
 It's been quite a while since we were aboard the Cosmopolitan.

6
00:02:12,770 --> 00:02:14,230
 We were only space cadets back then.

7
00:02:14,430 --> 00:02:15,610
 You remember those days, Psyche?

8
00:02:16,170 --> 00:02:17,480
 Say hello to me.

9
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:20,260
 It's a way to see you and nobody says hello.

10
00:02:21,730 --> 00:02:25,480
 Astro says that all my new circuits are the very latest.

11
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:27,920
 I am more extraordinary than ever. I am up to date.

12
00:02:28,210 --> 00:02:30,390
 I can't tell you how happy we are to see you.

13
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:32,770
 Now we're together again. We'll have a great time.

14
00:02:33,300 --> 00:02:35,580
 A little more dignity, I would suggest, Lieutenant.

15
00:02:36,270 --> 00:02:38,760
 Why haven't we taken off yet? We're late.

16
00:02:39,090 --> 00:02:43,050
 Ship is incapacitated. Circuit fusions in second motor propulsion group.

17
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:44,750
 There will be a delay.

18
00:02:45,530 --> 00:02:47,810
 Looks like we're going to miss the Cosmopolitan.

19
00:02:48,010 --> 00:02:50,630
 We're curious. We've never had anything go wrong in this service.

20
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:52,830
 Oh, it's always sabotage in the movies.

21
00:02:53,210 --> 00:02:54,940
 What good would that do anyway?

22
00:02:54,940 --> 00:02:58,970
 Well, my young friends, since you're going to miss the Cosmopolitan,

23
00:02:59,090 --> 00:03:01,790
 You can catch it on the next scheduled flight in ten days.

24
00:03:01,970 --> 00:03:04,070
 And there are still so many things I want to show you.

25
00:03:04,190 --> 00:03:05,890
 And anyway, I don't want to see you go.

26
00:03:05,910 --> 00:03:08,810
 No, I have a hunch we have to get on the Cosmopolitan now.

27
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:10,970
 I think we should do what Saki says.

28
00:03:11,050 --> 00:03:12,770
 My experience is her hunches aren't wrong.

29
00:03:13,350 --> 00:03:14,490
 What can we do, children?

30
00:03:14,710 --> 00:03:16,930
 It's going to leave soon, and we're stuck here.

31
00:03:17,510 --> 00:03:19,050
 Isn't there another shuttle vehicle?

32
00:03:19,490 --> 00:03:21,490
 Question already asked of Central Intelligence.

33
00:03:21,790 --> 00:03:23,390
 Reply, negative. Repeat, negative.

34
00:03:23,770 --> 00:03:27,190
 Will you tell me, my friend, how long is it going to take for the repairs to be carried out?

35
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:28,960
 Approximately two hours, sir.

36
00:03:29,390 --> 00:03:32,770
 Two hours? Really, that is a scandal. Have you ever heard of my exploits?

37
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:37,700
 This is ridiculous. Shuttle vehicle can catch the Cosmopolitan even after it is already left.

38
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:39,130
 What do you think?

39
00:03:39,310 --> 00:03:42,450
 The procedure is rather unusual, but I suppose it can be done.

40
00:03:51,520 --> 00:04:01,010
 Where are we?

41
00:04:01,010 --> 00:04:07,250
 We are traveling at maximum speed, sir. In a short while, we should make contact with that Cosmopolitan.

42
00:04:08,030 --> 00:04:11,530
 It was clever. He figured out we could catch it if we wanted to. Perfectly right.

43
00:04:12,180 --> 00:04:17,070
 Pilot, I want to send a signal to the ship. Ask them to open the entry hatch.

44
00:04:17,730 --> 00:04:24,630
 Yes, sir. This is Space Shuttle X-41. Request permission to come aboard spaceship Cosmopolitan.

45
00:04:25,230 --> 00:04:27,430
 Request access through the entry hatch.

46
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:32,520
 Shall I reply, Commodore?

47
00:04:33,250 --> 00:04:39,110
 Grant the request. Naturally, there can be no question of not authorizing them coming on board. Send the signal.

48
00:04:39,110 --> 00:04:42,600
 But see that Generex is detailed to their service and never leaves them.

49
00:05:01,090 --> 00:05:04,430
 My name is Generex. I have been assigned to your party.

50
00:05:05,390 --> 00:05:08,550
 And why, I would be glad to know I am here, in case you didn't notice.

51
00:05:08,870 --> 00:05:10,260
 What did you say?

52
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:11,630
 Nothing. Nothing.

53
00:05:13,170 --> 00:05:14,020
 No.

54
00:05:14,710 --> 00:05:21,820
 This is cabin number one. I suggest the gentlemen take it. The other, farther on, will be good for the lady.

55
00:05:22,730 --> 00:05:25,210
 Reserved adjoining cabins. That's what we want.

56
00:05:25,870 --> 00:05:34,800
 Done very well. Thank you, Generics. Now you can go.

57
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:39,670
 Negative, sir. I regret. My instructions are to remain with you at all times.

58
00:05:40,410 --> 00:05:47,510
 Yes, we appreciate your devotion to duty, but now will you be good enough to go away and right now get out of here, Generics. That's an order.

59
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:49,640
 You will dine at the Commodore's table.

60
00:05:52,510 --> 00:05:55,420
 Like this.

61
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:58,320
 The trip has tired you. You ought to rest, Psyche.

62
00:05:58,650 --> 00:05:59,620
 No, that's not it.

63
00:05:59,650 --> 00:06:02,560
 It's something else, only I don't know what it is.

64
00:06:02,850 --> 00:06:05,880
 I'll leave you. Metro will watch over you. You'll do that, won't you?

65
00:06:06,170 --> 00:06:10,160
 Of course I will, though I assure you I would rather rattle that bucket of bolts, Genevix.

66
00:06:10,300 --> 00:06:13,980
 I don't know if you noticed, but he's a lot larger than you are.

67
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:18,560
 And you, did you happen to notice when I entered into combat with a certain golden bar?

68
00:06:18,740 --> 00:06:20,400
 What was the result of that, I ask you?

69
00:06:20,830 --> 00:06:24,960
 Ladies and gentlemen, we now are passing through the asteroid belt.

70
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:27,890
 We urge you to admire the splendid view.

71
00:06:29,910 --> 00:06:42,330
 I was saying to Rudolph!

72
00:06:42,870 --> 00:06:45,230
 Ah, here come our slightly delayed heroes.

73
00:06:45,430 --> 00:06:45,690
 Welcome.

74
00:06:45,890 --> 00:06:48,390
 How long has it been since we last saw each other?

75
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:50,140
 Delighted you're here on our ship.

76
00:06:51,010 --> 00:06:53,050
 I would like to introduce the ship's officers.

77
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:55,500
 Our medical specialist, Professor Bernard.

78
00:06:55,970 --> 00:06:58,590
 Our data system specialist, Lieutenant Celia.

79
00:06:58,750 --> 00:06:59,990
 All her systems are go.

80
00:07:00,290 --> 00:07:05,440
 My second input, Captain Harold.

81
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:07,530
 Legal authority, Lori.

82
00:07:08,090 --> 00:07:10,840
 And to keep us on course, Chief Navigator Foster.

83
00:07:13,510 --> 00:07:15,780
 Lieutenant, you simply must tell us.

84
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:20,100
 How did you ever manage to turn away that rocket that would have destroyed the Earth?

85
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:21,820
 It must be a fascinating story.

86
00:07:21,840 --> 00:07:27,660
 Why don't you ask me to dance, Lieutenant?

87
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:33,260
 Madure, who is that man on my left there?

88
00:07:33,750 --> 00:07:36,990
 Ah, that's Foster. He's an excellent officer.

89
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:42,450
 He would have had command of a ship long ago if he hadn't this tendency to bend the elbow.

90
00:07:42,840 --> 00:07:46,680
 This ship was a floating desert. There wasn't anybody worth talking to.

91
00:07:47,010 --> 00:07:51,040
 Now the trip is going to be much more amusing. We'll have time to get acquainted, Lieutenant.

92
00:07:51,450 --> 00:07:56,350
 No, no, no. It's out of the question. I'm not letting them get away with this. I'll make them listen to me.

93
00:07:56,620 --> 00:07:58,940
 I hope you will come and see us on the bridge.

94
00:07:58,940 --> 00:08:02,970
 I know you will be interested in seeing all the new equipment and how it functions.

95
00:08:03,330 --> 00:08:05,250
 It's a far cry from your space bug.

96
00:08:05,410 --> 00:08:06,870
 That is from your hummingbird.

97
00:08:08,030 --> 00:08:10,490
 Well, it's like an insect when you compare it to this.

98
00:08:10,790 --> 00:08:12,410
 Yes, of course, hummingbird.

99
00:08:13,240 --> 00:08:16,910
 And who is it who has honored us only in absence?

100
00:08:17,190 --> 00:08:19,390
 Only a beautiful woman would do that, I'll bet.

101
00:08:19,710 --> 00:08:21,210
 You lose your bet, Lieutenant.

102
00:08:21,470 --> 00:08:24,210
 The empty chair is for Chief Mechanic Captain Walter.

103
00:08:24,650 --> 00:08:25,710
 Tonight he is not well.

104
00:08:26,340 --> 00:08:28,840
 I've noticed lately he is increasingly depressed.

105
00:08:28,940 --> 00:08:30,410
 I don't know what to do for him.

106
00:08:30,410 --> 00:08:33,560
 When space anxiety takes hold...

107
00:08:33,560 --> 00:08:34,360
 Want to dance?

108
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:36,830
 Can't tell you how much I'd like that, Lieutenant.

109
00:08:37,030 --> 00:08:37,890
 Well, what do you say?

110
00:08:40,170 --> 00:08:45,810
 We can talk here.

111
00:08:46,850 --> 00:08:49,080
 Glad you two are on this flight.

112
00:08:49,810 --> 00:08:53,300
 You and Lieutenant Pierre are the only people aboard that I dare talk to.

113
00:08:53,790 --> 00:08:55,670
 Crazy things are going on here.

114
00:08:55,950 --> 00:08:57,120
 It's hard to explain it.

115
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,990
 These new officers, the ones that got on at Port Doppler Hall,

116
00:09:02,230 --> 00:09:06,620
 they're good in their work, only can't get through it like they weren't there.

117
00:09:07,690 --> 00:09:11,110
 Surely it's not the first time you haven't taken to a new crew, is it?

118
00:09:11,350 --> 00:09:12,800
 No, but this is all together.

119
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:16,020
 These guys, I know, they give me the creeps.

120
00:09:16,140 --> 00:09:22,000
 And there's Chief Mechanic Walter. He's an old pal of mine, but now I think he's kind of flipping out a little.

121
00:09:22,390 --> 00:09:23,080
 And the Commodore?

122
00:09:23,660 --> 00:09:29,490
 Oh, I've known him for years, and he's acting kind of funny, too. I mean, I could be going nuts.

123
00:09:50,200 --> 00:09:56,130
 Spaceboat, how would he have known about that? And then he tried to cover it up. I have a feeling there's something very wrong here.

124
00:09:56,440 --> 00:09:58,140
 The Commodore, you think he's worried?

125
00:09:58,360 --> 00:10:06,170
 He's impossible to figure out. I tried to make some contact with him. No psychic communication worked with the Commodore. Nothing at all.

126
00:10:06,590 --> 00:10:10,060
 It's as if he was shielded. Nothing I tried got through to him.

127
00:10:10,320 --> 00:10:12,760
 They say that's what they teach in the third degree of telepathy.

128
00:10:13,080 --> 00:10:17,470
 But he's not telepathic at all. And when I try to...

129
00:10:17,470 --> 00:10:22,270
 Hears with microphones.

130
00:10:22,910 --> 00:10:24,370
 There you are, Lieutenant Pirro. Hello.

131
00:10:24,630 --> 00:10:27,610
 Are you trying to tell me that you're the Lieutenant Pirro from Omega?

132
00:10:28,250 --> 00:10:30,510
 No, you don't look like any big hero to me.

133
00:10:30,730 --> 00:10:32,160
 No, you'll never make me believe it.

134
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:33,460
 Hey, wait. Who is that man?

135
00:10:33,890 --> 00:10:35,230
 Walter. He is very ill.

136
00:10:44,340 --> 00:10:46,340
 You sure you understand?

137
00:10:47,240 --> 00:10:49,130
 Good lip. Do I look that dumb?

138
00:10:49,330 --> 00:10:51,580
 Don't get angry, old man. Now's not the time.

139
00:10:51,580 --> 00:11:02,350
 Why don't you sit down there? You two can share the co-pilot's chair.

140
00:11:02,860 --> 00:11:05,120
 There in front of us, Jupiter, we're getting nearer now.

141
00:11:05,780 --> 00:11:07,250
 As you can see, it's very beautiful.

142
00:11:07,530 --> 00:11:10,310
 Commodore, with all due respect, we are acquainted with the sector.

143
00:11:10,550 --> 00:11:13,040
 Oh, yes, of course, Sergeant. Yes, I was forgetting.

144
00:11:13,780 --> 00:11:16,470
 Message. We have intercepted a distress call.

145
00:11:16,630 --> 00:11:17,530
 All right, let's hear it.

146
00:11:17,890 --> 00:11:22,560
 Cargo spacecraft Jethro. Mayday. Mayday. Mayday.

147
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:25,790
 Destination Port Doppler. We have a mutiny on board.

148
00:11:26,140 --> 00:11:26,840
 Being attacked.

149
00:11:27,640 --> 00:11:30,780
 To the Jethro. This is the Cosmopolitan calling.

150
00:11:30,780 --> 00:11:35,370
 Message received. Indicate your position and tell us what we can do to help.

151
00:11:36,570 --> 00:11:40,970
 This is cargo spacecraft Jethro. Correction, the situation has returned to normal.

152
00:11:41,110 --> 00:11:45,010
 Our apology for that incorrect signal. Our radio operator went a little bit mad.

153
00:11:45,860 --> 00:11:48,720
 Disregard previous signal. Conditions normal.

154
00:11:49,060 --> 00:11:52,940
 Cosmopolitan, we will pass on your port side in approximately eight minutes. Out.

155
00:11:53,290 --> 00:11:56,050
 A curious business. Very curious. Right?

156
00:11:56,590 --> 00:11:59,460
 Yes, curious, all right. What do you think?

157
00:11:59,460 --> 00:12:04,030
 Cargo craft Jethro at 4 minutes

158
00:12:04,030 --> 00:12:06,640
 Projectory does not perform to data received

159
00:12:06,640 --> 00:12:09,540
 We will deviate 4 degrees to avoid oncoming craft

160
00:12:09,540 --> 00:12:11,930
 Have you any idea what's going on, Commodore?

161
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:12,880
 Hmm

162
00:12:12,880 --> 00:12:16,130
 Cargo craft Jethro at 2 minutes

163
00:12:16,130 --> 00:12:34,290
 Commodore, can't you get any closer?

164
00:12:34,660 --> 00:12:38,020
 You can't maneuver the cosmopolitan like a hummingbird, Lieutenant

165
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:39,460
 There are shuttlecraft

166
00:12:39,460 --> 00:12:40,940
 In case there's anyone alive

167
00:12:40,940 --> 00:12:43,880
 They would have ejected in their emergency modules

168
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:45,740
 At any moment, it could explode

169
00:12:45,740 --> 00:12:47,490
 Let us go and take a look

170
00:12:47,490 --> 00:12:49,980
 Yes, you have to let us go, it's your duty

171
00:12:50,500 --> 00:12:54,570
 You will ready a shuttle vehicle for Lieutenant Piero with the standard crew.

172
00:12:54,950 --> 00:12:55,630
 Part your immediately.

173
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:27,240
 Any survivors?

174
00:13:28,540 --> 00:13:30,940
 Furn or fiery coals, they didn't have a chance.

175
00:13:31,270 --> 00:13:32,150
 It's horrible.

176
00:13:32,620 --> 00:13:34,140
 How could such a terrible thing happen?

177
00:13:34,370 --> 00:13:34,920
 An accident.

178
00:13:35,430 --> 00:13:36,500
 An accidental explosion.

179
00:13:36,620 --> 00:13:36,880
 Anything.

180
00:13:37,150 --> 00:13:37,800
 Who knows?

181
00:13:38,160 --> 00:13:40,900
 And there was that strange message just before the tragedy.

182
00:13:41,580 --> 00:13:42,750
 Better go back.

183
00:13:43,100 --> 00:13:43,960
 What else can we do?

184
00:13:55,560 --> 00:14:04,990
 They are again our great heroes.

185
00:14:05,260 --> 00:14:09,610
 Hey, now are you ready to agree that this world is a little topsy-turvy?

186
00:14:09,610 --> 00:14:10,530
 Yeah, you agree, huh?

187
00:14:10,770 --> 00:14:12,670
 Hey, wait, wait, I want to tell you.

188
00:14:13,730 --> 00:14:18,590
 There's something wrong, Pierrot. And when I think of that accident...

189
00:14:18,590 --> 00:14:21,290
 We've both been depressed by that burning ship, naturally.

190
00:14:21,850 --> 00:14:24,950
 Not only that, no. It's not right. Things are going on.

191
00:14:25,220 --> 00:14:26,840
 And I just don't understand them.

192
00:14:27,300 --> 00:14:29,300
 Metro, did you do what I asked you to do?

193
00:14:29,590 --> 00:14:30,290
 Of course.

194
00:14:30,780 --> 00:14:31,200
 Well?

195
00:14:31,460 --> 00:14:33,080
 Well, I couldn't detect a thing.

196
00:14:33,540 --> 00:14:35,380
 They're not your run-of-the-mill android.

197
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:36,780
 Usually I can read them like a book.

198
00:14:37,210 --> 00:14:38,610
 These can resist all my race.

199
00:14:39,270 --> 00:14:41,220
 Exactly like humans, and yet...

200
00:14:41,220 --> 00:14:42,760
 And yet what, Metro?

201
00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:44,870
 Nothing I can put my feeler on.

202
00:14:44,870 --> 00:14:48,210
 and their answers are, well, too well organized, so to speak.

203
00:14:48,410 --> 00:14:50,370
 I shall need to go on studying them.

204
00:14:50,720 --> 00:14:52,460
 Hey, what are you two cooking up?

205
00:14:52,740 --> 00:14:56,910
 I don't say I especially like Commodore Robin, but all the same,

206
00:14:57,550 --> 00:15:00,320
 he's changed plenty since our first trip on the cosmos.

207
00:15:00,810 --> 00:15:01,690
 Remember, Psyche?

208
00:15:02,140 --> 00:15:05,280
 Sure, he's changed, but that could be because he's a little older.

209
00:15:05,600 --> 00:15:06,380
 Well, yes, maybe.

210
00:15:06,950 --> 00:15:09,770
 All right, and yet I tell you I'm sure there's something wrong.

211
00:15:09,950 --> 00:15:12,750
 I don't trust any of these men running the Cosmopolitan.

212
00:15:13,170 --> 00:15:15,450
 Only Foster, even if he's drunk all the time.

213
00:15:15,450 --> 00:15:16,570
 You're probably right.

214
00:15:17,440 --> 00:15:20,690
 Yeah, I think he was trying to tell us something a while ago.

215
00:15:21,350 --> 00:15:22,110
 You two stay here.

216
00:15:22,870 --> 00:15:25,710
 I'll see if I can find him and if there is something he wants to tell us.

217
00:15:26,140 --> 00:15:28,020
 These heroes are getting too curious.

218
00:15:28,330 --> 00:15:29,370
 They're starting to suspect.

219
00:15:29,790 --> 00:15:30,850
 They'll have to be watched.

220
00:15:31,030 --> 00:15:32,510
 I knew they'd be nothing but trouble.

221
00:15:32,710 --> 00:15:34,430
 You should never have left them come aboard.

222
00:15:34,690 --> 00:15:36,330
 We did all we could in that regard.

223
00:15:36,430 --> 00:15:38,730
 Now we have to cope with the situation as it is.

224
00:15:39,070 --> 00:15:41,270
 They could have a little accident, couldn't they?

225
00:15:41,530 --> 00:15:43,350
 No, they're too well known, too famous.

226
00:15:43,350 --> 00:15:48,050
 I remind you, Lieutenant, your mission consists in maintaining a close watch on this Lieutenant

227
00:15:48,050 --> 00:15:51,050
 up to now, you haven't been an unqualified success.

228
00:15:51,050 --> 00:15:53,050
 I will do my very best, don't worry.

229
00:15:53,050 --> 00:15:55,560
 As for you, Captain Generics,

230
00:15:55,560 --> 00:15:58,560
 you will use discretion in preventing undesirable actions

231
00:15:58,560 --> 00:16:00,560
 by Lieutenant Psyche and her android.

232
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:03,560
 You will avoid any unnecessary harshness if it's possible, if not then.

233
00:16:03,560 --> 00:16:07,550
 Commodore, it wouldn't take much to squash her and her tin can.

234
00:16:07,550 --> 00:16:08,550
 Do not be deceived, Captain.

235
00:16:08,550 --> 00:16:11,550
 Quite a number of others have paid for their overconfidence.

236
00:16:11,550 --> 00:16:13,550
 And what do we do with Captain Foster?

237
00:16:13,550 --> 00:16:16,790
 That one. Lately, he has become rather a nuisance.

238
00:16:16,790 --> 00:16:19,430
 You will have to liquidate the individual.

239
00:16:19,850 --> 00:16:21,310
 I count on you, Captain Harold.

240
00:16:27,530 --> 00:16:34,620
 You've been avoiding me, Lieutenant.

241
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:35,930
 You make me very sad.

242
00:16:36,480 --> 00:16:38,220
 You know that's not very nice, Lieutenant.

243
00:16:38,460 --> 00:16:40,580
 I thought you and I were getting on so well.

244
00:16:50,080 --> 00:17:00,000
 What do you want?

245
00:17:00,360 --> 00:17:02,370
 I think, Foster, we want to help you.

246
00:17:02,450 --> 00:17:03,250
 You're a little drunk.

247
00:17:03,480 --> 00:17:04,720
 Come on, follow us, Foster.

248
00:17:04,800 --> 00:17:05,340
 No trouble.

249
00:17:05,440 --> 00:17:06,260
 You just go along with us.

250
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:07,420
 No, no.

251
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:08,280
 Come, Foster.

252
00:17:08,460 --> 00:17:08,580
 No.

253
00:17:10,720 --> 00:17:13,080
 Oh, need help.

254
00:17:13,890 --> 00:17:28,160
 Having trouble again, as usual.

255
00:17:28,280 --> 00:17:31,050
 He was drunk and picked a fight with Captain Harold.

256
00:17:32,200 --> 00:17:33,220
 Seriously.

257
00:17:40,760 --> 00:17:47,220
 He has to be ready for tomorrow night.

258
00:17:47,220 --> 00:17:48,130
 He will be.

259
00:17:48,240 --> 00:17:51,750
 I don't know. I can't be sure. There are a lot of circuits here to be checked out.

260
00:17:52,120 --> 00:17:53,470
 Tomorrow night, Professor.

261
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,940
 All I can promise you, Commodore, is that I'll do my best.

262
00:18:00,020 --> 00:18:02,760
 Interesting, as good old Maestro always says.

263
00:18:06,250 --> 00:18:10,570
 Who do you want to visit?

264
00:18:11,260 --> 00:18:12,280
 Captain Foster.

265
00:18:12,590 --> 00:18:13,330
 Follow me.

266
00:18:13,810 --> 00:18:17,710
 He's taking a sleep cure. Any visitors would only interfere with that.

267
00:18:17,950 --> 00:18:19,720
 Just for one minute, that's all.

268
00:18:19,940 --> 00:18:25,140
 I told you any visits would only interfere with his treatment. I regret that I cannot authorize them.

269
00:18:29,670 --> 00:18:32,930
 Did you?

270
00:18:33,110 --> 00:18:37,040
 Pichol? That's what I mean. Now Foster's out of commission like Walters.

271
00:18:37,310 --> 00:18:39,290
 Metro, did you find out anything interesting?

272
00:18:39,630 --> 00:18:41,980
 Anything interesting? I don't know if it's interesting to you.

273
00:18:42,370 --> 00:18:47,570
 I can tell you Captain Harold contains a total of 25,801 electronic circuits.

274
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,800
 Whether or not you think that is something interesting, I can't say.

275
00:18:51,350 --> 00:18:52,320
 Are you sure?

276
00:18:52,540 --> 00:18:53,540
 They're robots, all of them.

277
00:18:53,900 --> 00:18:56,930
 In any case, he is one. A humanoid, to be exact.

278
00:18:57,260 --> 00:18:59,760
 And you are quite right, there are quite a few in this ship.

279
00:19:01,120 --> 00:19:06,820
 Just what I thought. Only these are a lot more sophisticated. You can't see into them.

280
00:19:06,820 --> 00:19:11,160
 Locate the real Captain Harold, and Foster, and the others.

281
00:19:11,320 --> 00:19:12,700
 I only hope it isn't too late.

282
00:19:13,180 --> 00:19:15,350
 But why are they doing all this? What is their aim?

283
00:19:15,630 --> 00:19:18,210
 If we can locate the others, they'll tell us what it is.

284
00:19:18,560 --> 00:19:21,790
 Only one thing is sure, we can't attack this giant ship, just the two of us.

285
00:19:22,370 --> 00:19:25,870
 Sorry, I should have said the three of us. We'll need more help.

286
00:19:26,500 --> 00:19:27,730
 Who else can we find?

287
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:30,470
 Look, we'll each go off and have a look around, okay?

288
00:19:31,510 --> 00:19:33,440
 Are these containers?

289
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:34,220
 Huh?

290
00:19:34,510 --> 00:19:37,840
 Yeah, this is where we store the spare parts for the ship in case repairs are needed.

291
00:19:41,680 --> 00:19:46,800
 Here, it's three degrees above freezing.

292
